Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 18, verse 33:

Since the trees may be spoiled because of the quality of their soil, He negated that by His words, the Exalted, as an answer to one who seems to have asked: "What is the state of their land, which produces the purity of their fruit?" (p-58) ﴿Each of the two gardens﴾, that is, each one of the two gardens mentioned, ﴿brought forth its produce﴾, that is, what is sought from them and eaten of fruit and grain, complete, with neither of them ascribed to any deficiency or inferiority. This is the meaning of ﴿and did not fall short﴾, that is, did not diminish, in the sensory or in the figurative sense, like one who places a thing in other than its proper place, ﴿of it in anything﴾.

Since the trees may be harmed in their continuance by a lack of watering, the Exalted said: ﴿And We caused a river to gush forth within them﴾, that is, a gushing befitting Our greatness, ﴿a river﴾, that is, one that extends and branches out so that it becomes like rivers, so that the freshness of the land endures and one is spared reliance on rain when drought occurs;
